Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 15:57:06 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] device: thread-group preemption when there is no compiler

Mid-thread preemption saves the threads' state with the SIP kernel,
which NEO gets from the compiler (IGC). Without a compiler library
Device::isStateSipRequired() is false, but PreemptionHelper's state SIP
programming asks for the SIP kernel when the mode is MidThread and
aborts (built_ins.cpp: no compiler interface) at the first launch on a
compute queue. A device whose default is MidThread now preempts at
thread-group boundaries when no compiler interface can be created: the
macOS port until IGC is ported. ForcePreemptionMode still overrides.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 shared/source/device/device.cpp | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/shared/source/device/device.cpp b/shared/source/device/device.cpp
index 0708f3fc14..2b2c6da238 100644
--- a/shared/source/device/device.cpp
+++ b/shared/source/device/device.cpp
@@ -174,6 +174,13 @@ bool Device::createDeviceImpl() {
         deferredImmediateCmdListEnabled = false;
     }
 
+    auto *mutableHwInfo = executionEnvironment->rootDeviceEnvironments[getRootDeviceIndex()]->getMutableHardwareInfo();
+    if (mutableHwInfo->capabilityTable.defaultPreemptionMode == PreemptionMode::MidThread && getCompilerInterface() == nullptr) {
+        // Mid-thread preemption saves the threads' state with the SIP kernel, which comes from the compiler (IGC).
+        // Without a compiler (the macOS port until IGC is ported) the device preempts at thread-group boundaries,
+        // instead of aborting at the first state SIP it would program.
+        mutableHwInfo->capabilityTable.defaultPreemptionMode = PreemptionMode::ThreadGroup;
+    }
     preemptionMode = PreemptionHelper::getDefaultPreemptionMode(getHardwareInfo());
 
     auto &productHelper = getProductHelper();
